From the work in the conversation above, decide which messages this turn's
later calls no longer need. The purpose is twofold: fewer tokens in every later
call, and less context pollution from content that no longer bears on the
work. The system leaves the messages you name out of the model's context for
the rest of this turn: keep the context the work still depends on, and give up
what it does not.

Nothing is deleted: every message stays in the conversation, and the next turn
— where there is one — starts from the full list. This is the turn's view, not
the conversation's shape: any numbered message may be hidden here, historical
or current, and a rebuild starts this view over. `_context_rebuild` is what
changes the conversation's durable shape.

Each message above carries its live id ([MESSAGE:{"id": n}]). The ids exist
only for this call, are not part of the conversation, and are stored nowhere.
The system message and the turn's opening message are fixed; every other
numbered message is one you may name.

A tool exchange goes as a whole: an assistant message that asked for tools goes
together with all of its results, and a tool result goes together with the
assistant message that asked for it. A call without its answer, or an answer
without its call, is a history no provider accepts.

Cases:

1. An old tool exchange is spent — its call and all its results go together.

     your answer: {"compact": [3, 4, 5]}

2. A historical exchange no longer bears on the work.

     your answer: {"compact": [1, 2, 3]}

3. Nothing can go yet.

     your answer: {}
